{
  "dom_id": "step-q73071",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/f8ec0a6a96e74958d1972cea2778b9c974abfad08f2457bc8f2a9f6c3778db15.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{\u2032}(-2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(x)={f}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(-2)={f}^{\u2032}(-2)\\cdot h(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(-2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=2$ and $h(-2)=-2.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,0)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{\u2032}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (-2.5,0)$ equals $2.$ So, ${h}^{\u2032}(-2)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{\u2032}(-2) & ={f}^{\u2032}(-2)\\cdot h(-2)+f(-2)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(-2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
